<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dien Bien Phu · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dien Bien Phu was fought in 1954 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> between the French army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vo-nguyen-giap">General Vo Nguyen Giap</a>. The Viet Minh besieged and captured the French base. The defeat ended French rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> and was a major blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu.jpg" alt="Battle of Dien Bien Phu" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vietnam People's Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victory_in_Battle_of_Dien_Bien_Phu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1953 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> had defeated a succession of French commanders, who had been unable to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> insurrection. In 1952 and 1953 the Viet Minh overran much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. Navarre looked for a way to protect it, and Colonel Louis Berteil formulated the hedgehog concept: a fortified airhead, supplied by air, beside Viet Minh supply lines to Laos.</p><p>In June 1953 Major General René Cogny proposed Dien Bien Phu, which had an old airstrip built by the Japanese. Navarre chose it, and every major subordinate officer protested; Cogny warned of a new Nà Sản under worse conditions. Operation Castor began on 20 November 1953, when the French dropped or flew about 9,000 troops into the area in three days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The French had assumed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> had no anti-aircraft capability. Under General Võ Nguyên Giáp the Viet Minh surrounded the camp and hauled heavy artillery, anti-aircraft guns among it, up the rear slopes of the mountains, where tunnels let them pull the guns out, fire and withdraw. Their attack began on 13 March 1954 against the outpost Béatrice. On 14 March the airstrip was put permanently out of commission, so supplies had to come by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a>. On 17 March most of the Tai troops defending Anne-Marie left or defected.</p><p>Fighting then centred on the strong points Eliane and Dominique, and from 5 April Giáp changed to entrenchment and sapping. By the end of April the outpost Isabelle was nearly out of water and ammunition. On 6 May the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> used Katyusha rockets for the first time and exploded a mine under Eliane 2. The garrison was overrun, and the survivors surrendered on 7 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Dien Bien Phu was the decisive battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, and the loss of the garrison cost roughly one-tenth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> manpower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> counted 11,721 prisoners on 8 May, of whom 4,436 were wounded. The able-bodied were force-marched over 600 km to prison camps, and hundreds died of disease on the way.</p><p>The Geneva Conference opened on 8 May, and its agreement in July partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> into a communist North and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> in the south, temporarily, with elections in 1956 that were never held. The last French forces left Vietnam in 1956. The defeat did not significantly alter the strategic situation, but it brought a deep psychological shock to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dien Bien Phu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese communist forces reached the border in 1949, and Chinese aid transformed the Viet Minh from a guerrilla militia into a standing army; with that help Võ Nguyên Giáp built a modern army that defeated the French at the <b>Battle of Dien Bien Phu</b> in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The catastrophic defeat that broke the second empire came at <b>Dien Bien Phu</b> in May 1954, where General Vo Nguyen Giap's Viet Minh besieged a French garrison and forced its surrender after fifty-six days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the First Indochina War from 1946, a French Union force of up to several hundred thousand men, including Vietnamese, Laotian and Cambodian troops, fought the Viet Minh, losing the frontier posts in 1950 and suffering final defeat at <b>Dien Bien Phu</b> in May 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat at <b>Dien Bien Phu</b> ended the Indochina war in 1954, and Pierre Mendès France negotiated the withdrawal.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-fourth-republic">French Fourth Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Viet Minh offensives overran much of the area in 1952 to 1953, and Lai Chau was evacuated before the battle of <b>Dien Bien Phu</b> in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/state/tai-federation">Tai Federation</a></li></ul></section>
